WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.480
Vsauce! Kevin here, with historical proof
that Isaac Newton was wrong at least once

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.880
in his storied career. He seems to have been
right about… gravity… and calculus…

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.800
and 58,000 other things that redefined how
humans thought about math and science. But

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.920
here’s what Newton couldn’t quite handle:

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:26.740
DICE.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:33.920
Some of ye are gonna think I’m wrong. And
others will agree that Newton was wrong but

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:39.120
ye will think that it doesn't even matter.
This will be fun.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.520
Let’s start by talkin’ about Pepys.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.720
Samuel Pepys gained fame as England’s Chief
Secretary to the Admiralty and eventually

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.640
became the 6th president of the Royal Society
of London -- the oldest national scientific

00:00:52.640 --> 00:01:00.080
institution in the world. But now he’s most
famous for his letters and meticulous diaries.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:06.160
In 1693, Pepys sent a dice gambling problem
to one of the most brilliant mathematical

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.160
minds alive: Sir Isaac Newton.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:16.000
It’s the only time Newton seems to have
worked on probability -- and even though he

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:24.720
was mostly right, he was still a little bit
wrong. Here’s how.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.520
Pepys made a wager with a man named John Smith
over which of the following scenarios was

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.840
the most likely to occur -- or if they were
all equal:

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.200
A - has 6 dice in a box, with which he is
to fling a 6;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:45.200
B - has in another box 12 dice, with which
he is to fling 2 sixes;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:51.440
C - has in another box 18 dice, with which
he is to fling 3 sixes.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:58.160
Q - Whether B and C have not as easy a task
as A at even luck?

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:05.680
Pepys’s problem isn’t hard to figure out…
until it is. On the surface, is it easier

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:15.600
to get one six in 6 dice, or three sixes in
18 dice? Or… is there no difference at all

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:24.000
in terms of probability? Pepys posited that
C, with three 6s in 18 dice, was the most

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.040
probable. More dice, better odds.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.880
Hey... What are we even doing with YE DICE
?

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:39.840
We’re using standard 6-sided dice with each
side equally likely as an outcome. No funny

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:47.600
business here. The odds of getting a 6 on
each individual roll are a simple 1 in 6.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.320
Now we just need to “fling” them.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:58.640
WAIT -- we’ve got a language problem. Is
the bet here to find out whether we’re flinging

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:09.040
a 6, or does that imply at least one 6? Because
exactly one 6 and at least one 6 are two different

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:16.000
things, and that would apply to B and C, too.
It’s a critical point in figuring out exactly

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.200
what our odds are and whether there’s any
difference. As they exchanged letters, Newton

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:28.080
interpreted Pepys’ problem as being satisfied
with at least the minimum number of sixes.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.680
And while Pepys predicted that the best shot
to win was rolling at least three 6s in a

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:41.840
fling of 18 dice, Newton proved it was the
opposite: A is most likely, B is next most

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:48.960
likely, and “In ye third case, ye value
will be found still less.”

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:55.360
And I know that people don’t believe this.
There are articles written to this day, 300

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:02.480
years later, claiming that the probability
does not change when ye add dice. After all,

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:09.600
every die has that 1 in 6 chance of coming
up 6… and a set of 6 dice is really just

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:17.440
6 individual dice. 12 and 18? That’s just
two and three sets of 6. But they are wrong!

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.360
And I’ll show ye why.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:23.920
It’s time to prove this by flinging some
dice. Mathematically.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:30.640
All the possible probabilities of an event
need to add up to 1 -- so in a coin flip,

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:37.840
there’s a .5 probability of heads and a
.5 probability of tails -- and the probability

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:45.840
of one of those two outcomes occurring is
.5 + .5 = 1, because one of those two things

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.000
is certain to happen. It’s the same with
our dice game.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.280
So to find out the probability of something
happening, it’s usually best to calculate

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:01.680
the probability of that thing not happening
and then subtracting it from 1. Those are

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:08.400
the only two outcomes here, it happens or
it doesn’t, so that calculation “everything

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.640
minus failure” leaves us with the probability
of success.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:20.240
The probability of rolling a 6 is p... Hold
on I gotta find my marker. Probability of

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:29.680
finder my marker -- 100%! The probability
of rolling a 6 is p. So p = ⅙. The probability

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:39.840
of not rolling a 6 is (1 - p), so the probability
of not rolling a 6 with 6 dice -- our A scenario

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.280
-- is 1 - (1-p)^6.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:50.800
Plug in the ⅙ value for p and the probability
of rolling at least one 6 with just 6 dice

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.600
= .6651…

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.800
We do the same thing for B. It’s not any
more sophisticated, there are just more terms…

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:08.000
and a little extra step, since rolling only
one 6 in 12 dice is now considered a failure,

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.480
too. So the probability of B equals:

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:17.520
B = 1 - (1-p)^12 - (12/1)p(1 - (1-p)^11

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.960
That simplifies to: 1 - (1+11p)(1-p)^11

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:34.720
… and when we put in our ⅙ value again,
the probability of succeeding in scenario

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.800
B is .6187…

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:44.560
It’s about 5 percentage points less than
the odds of winning with only 6 dice. And

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:48.320
that’s actually about a 7% swing in outcomes.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:55.600
And like Newton said, C is even worse: “ye
value will be found still less” turned out

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:01.120
to be about .5973.
But Newton didn’t actually calculate the

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:07.520
third scenario. He had to calculate all of
this math by hand -- not using the simple

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:13.280
probability notation we just used -- and he
actually made a really detailed series of

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:19.760
tables that Pepys could use to do the calculations
himself. Newton’s tables used first principles

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:25.280
-- basic knowledge rather than complicated,
specialized knowledge -- so Pepys could confirm

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:31.680
the calculations like this and plug in his
own values. So for example, the probability

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:38.320
of at least one “6” in a toss of 6 dice
is obtained from column F by dividing row

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:44.960
9, “outcomes with at least one ‘6’”
by row 2, “total outcomes for the dice thrown.”

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:51.360
It’s simple, elegant genius. I, and ye,
can do it a lot more simply and crunch the

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:58.560
numbers on a calculator, or run a simulation
of, say, 100,000 trials of 18 dice-rolls,

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:07.280
in just a few seconds to confirm the result.
But if ye want tables like Pepys, ye got tables.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:13.120
Isaac Newton didn’t have those computational
tools, and to be honest, he just wasn’t

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:19.760
that familiar with probability. No one in
the 17th century was. And that’s why he

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:23.920
got the numbers right, but he got the reasoning
wrong.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.560
In 2006, Stephen Stigler,&nbsp;
a professor of statistics&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.160
at the University of Chicago, detailed a serious

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:38.960
flaw in Newton’s logic. As the correspondence
evolved, Newton assigned Scenario A to a fictional

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:46.960
man named Peter and Scenario B to James. He
explained that Peter only needed one 6 in

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:54.160
his fling of 6 dice, while James needed a
6 in each fling of 6 dice -- so, it was easier

00:08:54.160 --> 00:09:02.960
for Peter to win. Same with the unnamed Player
C, who’s flinging 18 dice. Newton completely

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:09.920
ignored the possibility of getting more than
one 6 in each fling, and that’s just…

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:14.880
a different problem than the one we’re dealing
with here.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:22.320
If that seems like a minor detail, it really
isn’t. Yeah, his explanation was wrong but

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:29.280
his answer was right. And that seems to be
enough for most people. Almost no one talks

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:36.880
about how his conclusion was wrong. And that’s
what’s amazing about this problem to me.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:43.440
Does it matter if ye are wrong about why ye
are right?

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:50.480
On December 23, 1693, in his third letter
on Pepys’ dice problem, Newton’s explanation

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:56.160
conflated that original language problem I
pointed out -- whether we’re hoping to get

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.560
exactly a number of 6s, or at least a certain
number.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:08.880
He was confused. He knew the math and he nailed
it, despite probabilistic thinking not even

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:16.000
really being a thing in his era… but his
reasoning couldn’t quite catch up to the

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:16.500
numbers.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:18.480
That’s okay.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:26.160
And for centuries, we kinda let that go. Part
of it was that it took a long time to develop

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:33.680
enough knowledge to know why Newton’s explanation
wasn’t exactly right. Probability seems

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:37.600
to have evolved from wanting&nbsp;
a better understanding&nbsp;

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:41.200
of gambling, and the numbers were accurate.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:48.560
And part of it was that… he’s Isaac Newton.
No one’s making a career off disputing the

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:56.640
details of a man on humanity’s Mount Rushmore
of math and science. Except Stigler. And I

00:10:57.680 --> 00:10:58.920
guess… me.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:06.400
But the Newton-Pepys problem shows us how
numbers can’t just exist on their own. They

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:13.520
work hand-in-hand with our understanding of
the problems they’re applied to. And really,

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:19.680
Newton’s solution is evidence that Occam’s
Razor is right: that the simplest method is

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:27.120
probably the right one, and the simpler sequence
of events is probably going to work more often.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:33.120
We use that mindset to develop and improve
everything from manufacturing and assembly

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:39.840
lines to coding software for sustainability.
We need it to progress.

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:48.400
The important thing is someone needs to get
complicated about getting simple to prevent

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:57.200
seemingly simple things from suddenly getting…
complicated. Which makes things simpler for

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.880
us so that we can move on to things that are
more complicated.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:09.840
And as always -- thanks for watching.

